message.content:
Under the null hypothesis \(H_0\) you assume each trial has a fixed probability  
\(p_{\text{null}} = 0.1\) of being a “success”.  

For a binomial experiment with \(n = 500\) independent trials, the **expected** (mean) number of successes is simply

\[
E[\text{\# successes}] \;=\; n\,p_{\text{null}}
   \;=\; 500 \times 0.1
   \;=\; 50 .
\]

If you prefer to express this as a proportion rather than a raw count:

\[
E[\hat{p}]
    \;=\;
    E\!\left[ \frac{\text{\# successes}}{n}\right]
    \;=\;
    \frac{E[\text{\# successes}]}{n}
    \;=\;
    \frac{50}{500}
    \;=\; 0.10 .
\]

So, according to the null hypothesis, we would expect on average **50 successes** (i.e., a success rate of **10 %**) over those 500 trials.
